1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:05,000
{\an8}♪ ♪

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:09,988
<i>There was a table set out under
a tree in front of the house,</i>

3
00:00:10,138 --> 00:00:13,832
<i>and the March Hare and the
Hatter were having tea at it:</i>

4
00:00:13,982 --> 00:00:17,264
<i>a Dormouse was sitting
between them, fast asleep,</i>

5
00:00:17,414 --> 00:00:22,108
<i>and the other two were using it as a
cushion, resting their elbows on it,</i>

6
00:00:22,258 --> 00:00:24,187
<i>and talking over its head.</i>

7
00:00:24,337 --> 00:00:26,854
Very uncomfortable for the Dormouse,

8
00:00:27,004 --> 00:00:30,227
only, as it’s asleep,
I suppose it doesn’t mind.

9
00:00:30,677 --> 00:00:34,429
<i>The table was a large one,
but the three were all crowded</i>

10
00:00:34,579 --> 00:00:36,626
<i>together at one corner of it</i>

11
00:00:36,776 --> 00:00:38,176
No room! No room!

12
00:00:38,326 --> 00:00:40,078
There’s plenty of room!

13
00:00:40,228 --> 00:00:44,039
<i>She sat down in a large arm-chair
at one end of the table.</i>

14
00:00:44,489 --> 00:00:45,771
Have some wine,

15
00:00:46,221 --> 00:00:50,738
<i>Alice looked all round the table,
but there was nothing on it but tea.</i>

16
00:00:50,888 --> 00:00:53,464
- I don’t see any wine,
- There isn’t any,

17
00:00:53,914 --> 00:00:56,961
Then it wasn’t very civil
of you to offer it,

18
00:00:57,411 --> 00:01:01,458
It wasn’t very civil of you to
sit down without being invited,

19
00:01:01,908 --> 00:01:04,190
I didn’t know it was your table,

20
00:01:04,340 --> 00:01:07,269
it’s laid for a great
many more than three.

21
00:01:07,719 --> 00:01:09,530
Your hair wants cutting,

22
00:01:09,680 --> 00:01:13,844
<i>He had been looking at Alice for
some time with great curiosity,</i>

23
00:01:13,994 --> 00:01:16,158
<i>and this was his first speech.</i>

24
00:01:16,608 --> 00:01:19,713
You should learn not to
make personal remarks,

25
00:01:19,863 --> 00:01:21,145
it’s very rude.

26
00:01:21,595 --> 00:01:26,289
<i>The Hatter opened his eyes very wide
on hearing this; but all he said was</i>

27
00:01:26,439 --> 00:01:28,897
Why is a raven like a writing-desk?

28
00:01:29,347 --> 00:01:31,688
Come, we shall have some fun now!

29
00:01:31,838 --> 00:01:36,120
I’m glad they’ve begun asking riddles.
—I believe I can guess that,

30
00:01:36,570 --> 00:01:40,558
Do you mean that you think you
can find out the answer to it?

31
00:01:41,008 --> 00:01:42,208
Exactly so,

32
00:01:42,658 --> 00:01:45,058
Then you should say what you mean,

33
00:01:45,508 --> 00:01:46,708
I do,

34
00:01:46,858 --> 00:01:51,258
at least—at least I mean what I say—
that’s the same thing, you know.

35
00:01:51,708 --> 00:01:53,578
Not the same thing a bit!

36
00:01:53,728 --> 00:01:57,775
You might just as well say that
‘I see what I eat’ is the same

37
00:01:57,925 --> 00:02:01,560
- thing as ‘I eat what I see’!
- You might just as well say,

38
00:02:01,710 --> 00:02:05,992
that ‘I like what I get’ is the
same thing as ‘I get what I like’!

39
00:02:06,442 --> 00:02:08,430
You might just as well say,

40
00:02:08,580 --> 00:02:13,450
that ‘I breathe when I sleep’ is the
same thing as ‘I sleep when I breathe’!

41
00:02:13,900 --> 00:02:16,064
It is the same thing with you,

42
00:02:16,214 --> 00:02:20,672
<i>Here the conversation dropped, and
the party sat silent for a minute,</i>

43
00:02:20,822 --> 00:02:25,869
<i>while Alice thought over all she could
remember about ravens and writing-desks,</i>

44
00:02:26,019 --> 00:02:27,477
<i>which wasn’t much.</i>

45
00:02:27,927 --> 00:02:29,974
What day of the month is it?

46
00:02:30,124 --> 00:02:33,994
<i>Turning to Alice: he had taken
his watch out of his pocket,</i>

47
00:02:34,144 --> 00:02:38,191
<i>and was looking at it uneasily,
shaking it every now and then,</i>

48
00:02:38,341 --> 00:02:40,270
<i>and holding it to his ear.</i>

49
00:02:40,720 --> 00:02:42,649
- The fourth.
- Two days wrong!

50
00:02:42,799 --> 00:02:45,669
I told you butter wouldn’t suit the works!

51
00:02:46,119 --> 00:02:47,871
It was the best butter,

52
00:02:48,321 --> 00:02:51,426
Yes, but some crumbs
must have got in as well,

53
00:02:51,576 --> 00:02:54,917
you shouldn’t have put it
in with the bread-knife.

54
00:02:55,367 --> 00:02:59,061
<i>The March Hare took the watch
and looked at it gloomily:</i>

55
00:02:59,211 --> 00:03:03,258
<i>then he dipped it into his cup
of tea, and looked at it again:</i>

56
00:03:03,408 --> 00:03:07,631
<i>but he could think of nothing
better to say than his first remark</i>

57
00:03:07,781 --> 00:03:10,122
It was the best butter, you know.

58
00:03:10,572 --> 00:03:14,560
<i>Alice had been looking over his
shoulder with some curiosity.</i>

59
00:03:14,710 --> 00:03:16,227
What a funny watch!

60
00:03:16,377 --> 00:03:20,718
It tells the day of the month,
and doesn’t tell what o’clock it is!

